The administration of this Ordinance shall be the responsibility of the Woodstock Municipal Planning Commission. The provisions of this article are intended as legal guidelines and requirements for the establishment and operation of the administration of this Ordinance.
The review and decision of the Planning Commission and City Council shall be made in accordance with the provisions of Chapter XI of this Ordinance.
1. Permits for Mobile Home Parks. In order to obtain a development permit for a mobile home park, the applicant shall first submit an application and plan to the City Staff according to the following procedures:
a. Pre-Application Review. Prior to the submittal of a preliminary plan for the development of a mobile home park, the applicant shall meet with the City Staff for a review of the location, slope, and nature of the proposed mobile home park.
b. Submission of Preliminary Plan. Prior to issuance of a land-disturbance or construction permit, the developer shall submit a preliminary plan prepared in accordance with the provisions of this Ordinance to the City Planning staff, at least ten (10) business days prior to the regular meeting date of the Planning Commission. The preliminary plan shall be drawn to scale or one (1) inch equals one hundred (100) feet and include the following information:
□ Name of proposed park: as well as the name and address of owner and developer;
□ Date prepared, north arrow, land lot lines, angles, bearing and distances, location and dimensions of proposed streets, mobile home lots, easements, buildings, parking, recreation areas, yards, and other open spaces;
□ The boundary line of the tract to be developed with accurate linear and angular dimensions;
□ Sketch or city map showing general location of the proposed mobile home park;
□ Topography by contours at vertical intervals of not more than five (5) feet;
□ The location of existing and platted property lines, streets, buildings, water courses, railroads, bridges, water mains, sewers, culverts, drain pipe, and public utility easements, both on the land to be developed and on that portion of the land immediately adjoining which abuts the land to be developed, and any other pertinent characteristics of the land;
□ Names of adjoining subdivisions or the names of record: as well as all adjoining parcels of unsubdivided land;
□ Utility and drainage plans shall be provided including all information required by the County Health Department, the City Engineer, and City Building Inspector to determine the water, sewer, electricity, refuse collection, and storm drainage improvements will be made and located in accordance with the requirements of this Ordinance;
□ The location, dimensions, and treatment of all required buffer or screening areas; and
□ Other information required by the Woodstock Municipal Planning Commission to insure compliance with the provisions of this Ordinance.
3. Approval/Disapproval of Preliminary Plan. The Planning Commission shall have thirty (30) days from the date that the completed application is submitted for review, to recommend to the Woodstock City Council approval or disapproval of the preliminary plan for the proposed mobile home park. Failure of the Planning Commission to act within thirty (30) days shall be deemed approval of such plan.
4. Submission of Final Plan. Approval of the preliminary plan by the Woodstock City Council shall authorize the developer to proceed with actual construction and development of the proposed mobile home park. Upon completion of all required improvements, and certification by the City Clerk that surety in a sufficient amount to assure completion of all required improvements has been received, the developer shall submit copies of a final plan for review and approval by the Planning Commission. The final plan shall conform substantially to the preliminary plan and shall meet all requirements of this Ordinance. The Planning Commission shall follow the procedures in Chapter XI for review and approval of the final plan.
The final plan shall also include the following:
a. Certification by a registered engineer or surveyor attesting to the accuracy of the plan;
b. Certification by the Cherokee County Health Department that the water and sewerage systems fully meet the requirements of the Health Department;
c. Certification by the City Engineer that all streets and storm drainage improvements have been installed, and surety provided therefore, according to the plan; and
d. Certification of approval by the Chairman of the Woodstock Municipal Planning Commission.
5. Approval of Final Plan. Within thirty (30) days after submission of the final plan together with all required supporting data and certifications, the Planning Commission shall review and approve or disapprove the plan. Failure of the Planning Commission to act within thirty (30) days shall be deemed approval of such plan.
6. Development Compliance. At least one hundred (100) percent of each phase of all planned spaces, as well as all streets, utilities, and other required improvements necessary to serve them pursuant to these regulations must be completely constructed according to the mobile home park plan approved by the Planning Commission before the fist mobile home location permit will be issued.
Failure of the developer or applicant to construct this minimum phase within two (2) years after approval by the Planning Commission shall cause such approval to expire.

